1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:10,000
When we finally encounter an extraterrestrial life form, will we study it under a microscope?

2
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Or get to shake hands? Either way, it's bound to change our lives.

3
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:19,000
Now, ET Tech, the scientific search for extraterrestrial life on Modern Marvels.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,080
In Puerto Rico, the world's largest radio telescope aims at a distant star.

5
00:00:36,060 --> 00:00:38,020
Scientists monitor it around the clock,

6
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:41,260
hoping to detect an artificially generated radio signal,

7
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:44,540
evidence of an extraterrestrial civilization.

8
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:48,740
At the Lick Observatory near San Jose, California,

9
00:00:49,520 --> 00:00:52,400
astronomers search the heavens for a high-powered light beacon,

10
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:57,040
a calling card beamed by extraterrestrials from a solar system

11
00:00:57,041 --> 00:00:58,840
trillions of miles from Earth.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,780
Meanwhile, four NASA space probes launched in the 1970s,

13
00:01:05,960 --> 00:01:07,800
carrying information about our planet,

14
00:01:08,380 --> 00:01:11,220
continue their journey to worlds far beyond our solar system.

15
00:01:12,180 --> 00:01:15,020
These are a few of the ways that astronomers have been searching

16
00:01:15,021 --> 00:01:19,000
for intelligent extraterrestrial life, or E.T. himself.

17
00:01:19,920 --> 00:01:22,320
But just what do these searchers consider intelligent?

18
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:27,060
If you can build a radio transmitter or a high-powered laser so that we can detect you,

19
00:01:27,340 --> 00:01:29,400
then from our point of view, you're intelligent.

20
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,700
While encountering intelligent life might be the pinnacle moment in human history,

21
00:01:35,620 --> 00:01:41,060
scientists are no less eager to encounter extraterrestrials that can only be seen under a microscope.

22
00:01:42,060 --> 00:01:46,500
Let's say if we go to Mars and we find evidence of microbial life,

23
00:01:47,160 --> 00:01:49,680
there are actually two things that could mean.

24
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:55,640
One is that life on Mars and Earth are related.

25
00:01:56,200 --> 00:01:58,540
Somehow they're products of the same event.

26
00:01:58,541 --> 00:02:02,800
And as time went by, Mars could no longer sustain life.

27
00:02:03,460 --> 00:02:07,160
And so we're looking, maybe looking at ancient distant cousin.

28
00:02:07,760 --> 00:02:13,040
The other possibility is it started of its own origin, not of Earth, but of Mars itself.

29
00:02:13,480 --> 00:02:20,740
Now that has the possibility of giving us a whole other example of what life is like.

30
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:27,620
The quest to discover if life exists on Mars has been a great scientific drama for more than 100 years.

31
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:33,460
In June 2003, the next act would involve a nine-year-old girl,

32
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:38,200
the winner of a nationwide essay contest to name two robotic explorers

33
00:02:38,201 --> 00:02:40,460
headed to Mars in search of life.

34
00:02:40,461 --> 00:02:46,400
She has in her heritage and upbringing the soul of two great space-faring countries, to be sure.

35
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:50,760
Sophie Collis was adopted by an American family from an orphanage in Siberia.

36
00:02:51,980 --> 00:02:55,840
At night, I looked up at the sparkly sky.

37
00:02:56,420 --> 00:02:57,360
I felt better.

38
00:02:57,620 --> 00:03:04,620
I dreamed I could fly there. In America, I can make all my dreams come true.

39
00:03:04,621 --> 00:03:08,620
Thank you for the spirit and the opportunity.

40
00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:17,620
Spirit and Opportunity are twin 400-pound solar-powered geologists.

41
00:03:17,621 --> 00:03:20,620
If all goes well on their seven-month long journeys,

42
00:03:20,621 --> 00:03:26,620
they will land on Mars to conduct life-seeking missions in January 2004.

43
00:03:27,620 --> 00:03:35,200
They are the larger, more technologically advanced offspring of the Sojourner rover that explored Mars in 1997.

44
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:42,060
NASA scientists have chosen landing sites for spirit and opportunity where they think water might have once existed.

45
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:45,720
Because where there's water, there could be life.

46
00:03:45,820 --> 00:03:49,340
There may be boundaries of grains could contain liquid water.

47
00:03:49,960 --> 00:03:54,120
And little micro-environments, micro-climate areas, could form.

48
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:55,680
They could be niches.

49
00:03:56,300 --> 00:03:58,460
Martian life, if it exists, and we don't know,

50
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:04,560
might actually have found pathways to go there and live in those tiny environments.

51
00:04:05,340 --> 00:04:08,380
But before Spirit and Opportunity could visit Mars,

52
00:04:08,940 --> 00:04:13,920
they had to pass a cleanliness test given by a scientist with an out-of-this-world title,

53
00:04:14,980 --> 00:04:16,940
the Planetary Protection Officer.

54
00:04:17,400 --> 00:04:22,580
I work to make sure that we don't contaminate other planets with biological materials from Earth,

55
00:04:22,581 --> 00:04:25,320
and that if we bring something back from another planet,

56
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:28,940
we check it for life before we distribute it widely here on Earth.

57
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:34,600
Rummel and his team were involved in the earliest stages of the Mars mission

58
00:04:34,601 --> 00:04:38,200
to assure both rovers were built under the cleanest of conditions.

59
00:04:39,480 --> 00:04:43,240
Some parts were even sterilized through oven baking prior to assembly.

60
00:04:44,280 --> 00:04:48,060
Then, as Spirit and Opportunity were going through final launch preparations,

61
00:04:48,260 --> 00:04:52,180
the protection team continually collected culture samples from the rovers.

62
00:04:52,580 --> 00:04:55,580
making sure they were clean enough to go to Mars.

63
00:04:55,581 --> 00:04:58,580
You may look at a tabletop and not see the life that's there,

64
00:04:58,581 --> 00:05:00,580
but in fact it's very abundant.

65
00:05:00,581 --> 00:05:05,580
And a typical tabletop in an office is way too dirty to be sent to Mars.

66
00:05:05,581 --> 00:05:09,580
While it's virtually impossible to remove every single microorganism,

67
00:05:09,581 --> 00:05:15,580
the acceptable limit for Spirit and Opportunity is 300,000 aerobic spores.

68
00:05:15,581 --> 00:05:19,580
That's about the same amount of microbial life as you would find

69
00:05:19,581 --> 00:05:23,600
and half a bottle of water that you'd buy in a machine or at the store.

70
00:05:23,980 --> 00:05:25,520
Very small bottle of water, in fact.

71
00:05:26,500 --> 00:05:29,460
When Spirit was determined to be well within those parameters,

72
00:05:30,040 --> 00:05:34,260
it was encased inside the capsule-shaped spacecraft that will carry it to Mars.

73
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:37,520
It was then transported to the launch pad,

74
00:05:38,460 --> 00:05:40,520
lifted more than 100 feet off the ground,

75
00:05:41,620 --> 00:05:46,880
and secured inside the fairing, or nose cone, of a three-stage Delta II rocket.

76
00:05:46,881 --> 00:05:57,500
Four, three, two, one, engine start, and liftoff of the Delta II rocket carrying the Spirit from Earth to planet Mars.

77
00:05:59,520 --> 00:06:02,680
And there's jettison, solid motor jettison.

78
00:06:04,400 --> 00:06:11,340
NASA Animation illustrates how both it and Opportunity, launched on July 7th, will land and work on Mars.

79
00:06:12,300 --> 00:06:17,980
37 minutes after launch, the capsule containing the rover separates from the rocket's third stage.

80
00:06:18,660 --> 00:06:24,260
It's attached to a cruise stage, equipped with solar panels, which provide the power to reach Mars.

81
00:06:25,340 --> 00:06:29,020
Seven months later, the spacecraft approaches the Red Planet.

82
00:06:30,260 --> 00:06:34,620
15 minutes before entering the Martian atmosphere, the cruise stage is jettisoned.

83
00:06:34,621 --> 00:06:39,420
The capsule hits the atmosphere at 12,000 miles per hour,

84
00:06:39,740 --> 00:06:42,340
protected by a heat shield like the ones used on Apollo.

85
00:06:43,600 --> 00:06:47,160
Here we are. We're coming straight in, faster than a speeding bullet.

86
00:06:47,540 --> 00:06:51,940
We hit the Mars atmosphere, and we have six minutes to roll to a stop and then call home.

87
00:06:52,180 --> 00:06:54,580
Six minutes, as many of us say, of terror.

88
00:06:55,860 --> 00:07:00,220
Five miles above the surface, a parachute deploys, slowing the descent.

89
00:07:04,620 --> 00:07:13,500
Twenty seconds later, the heat shield drops off, and the rover, encased in a deflated airbag, spools out on a 66-foot-long bridle.

90
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:19,220
Eight seconds from crashing into the surface, the airbags rapidly inflate.

91
00:07:21,140 --> 00:07:25,920
Immediately afterwards, the capsule fires retro rockets, bringing it to a near stop.

92
00:07:26,040 --> 00:07:33,980
So we will cut the bridle, and now drop to the surface of Mars, hitting the surface at something like 25 to 40 Gs,

93
00:07:33,981 --> 00:07:36,340
enough to black out a human being, but not our rovers.

94
00:07:37,200 --> 00:07:42,100
The rover, nestled inside its cocoon, begins bouncing across Mars,

95
00:07:42,640 --> 00:07:46,280
perhaps covering a full mile before finally rolling to a stop.

96
00:07:47,040 --> 00:07:49,060
The airbags deflate and retract.

97
00:07:50,040 --> 00:07:54,220
Landing pedals open up in such a way that the rover will be turned right side up,

98
00:07:54,560 --> 00:07:56,240
regardless of its position on landing.

99
00:07:57,680 --> 00:08:01,400
Next, the rover unfolds its power-generating solar panels

100
00:08:01,401 --> 00:08:05,720
and deploys a mast on which panoramic and navigational cameras are attached.

101
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:10,820
They provide controllers on Earth, a set of eyes on Mars.

102
00:08:11,420 --> 00:08:14,820
And if all goes well, we will receive the signals from those rovers

103
00:08:14,821 --> 00:08:22,980
and begin a type of expedition that will be previously unexperienced in our human condition.

104
00:08:23,660 --> 00:08:26,560
The rover then rises up from a crouched position,

105
00:08:27,180 --> 00:08:30,640
unfolds its wheels from beneath it, and rolls off the landing pad.

106
00:08:31,400 --> 00:08:39,040
It uses infrared vision to help scientists decide which rock or patch of soil is of greatest interest to them.

107
00:08:40,800 --> 00:08:46,880
Both Spirit and Opportunity are capable of traveling up to 44 yards in any direction of the planet's surface daily.

108
00:08:49,080 --> 00:08:52,700
They're also both equipped with a suite of science instruments.

109
00:08:55,480 --> 00:08:59,560
Among other things, they can grind away the outer surface of a rock,

110
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:02,020
take a microscopic view inside it,

111
00:09:02,500 --> 00:09:04,640
and then make a chemical analysis of it.

112
00:09:05,700 --> 00:09:09,020
It's inside the rocks where clues about life might be hidden.

113
00:09:09,680 --> 00:09:13,860
If some of the rocks are chocked full of the stuff of life,

114
00:09:14,040 --> 00:09:17,140
molecules involving carbon and hydrogen and oxygen and nitrogen,

115
00:09:17,460 --> 00:09:21,460
above certain thresholds, these rovers could see those chemicals.

116
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:23,100
And as I like to tell everyone,

117
00:09:23,500 --> 00:09:26,500
I've never met a rock that lied to me, if you can read them right.

118
00:09:27,100 --> 00:09:29,200
So we have the tools to read the rocks.

119
00:09:29,560 --> 00:09:36,540
Though it's possible for Spirit and Opportunity to discover life on Mars, scientists aren't overly optimistic.

120
00:09:37,400 --> 00:09:44,620
But they do hope the rover's geological research will help clarify where and how to search for life in future missions.

121
00:09:46,120 --> 00:09:53,820
While Spirit and Opportunity represent an apex in the scientific search for extraterrestrial life, the roots of that search are ancient.

122
00:09:54,820 --> 00:09:58,100
We can't really know when one of our ancestors looked to the stars

123
00:09:58,101 --> 00:10:00,960
and wondered if they might glimpse a sign of life.

124
00:10:01,840 --> 00:10:03,840
But in the late 1800s,

125
00:10:03,841 --> 00:10:07,180
an amateur astronomer would fuel the public's imagination

126
00:10:07,181 --> 00:10:09,520
with his theories on extraterrestrial life,

127
00:10:09,820 --> 00:10:14,300
and we've been obsessed with Martians and UFOs ever since.

128
00:10:15,840 --> 00:10:18,600
Spirit and Opportunity each carry a DVD

129
00:10:18,601 --> 00:10:22,780
containing the names of 3.5 million people collected during NASA's

130
00:10:22,781 --> 00:10:24,840
Send your name to Mars campaign.

131
00:10:25,360 --> 00:10:27,560
E.T. Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

132
00:10:30,220 --> 00:10:33,780
We now return to E.T. Tech on Modern Marvels.

133
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:38,880
Since the invention of the telescope in 1608,

134
00:10:39,700 --> 00:10:43,200
countless amateur and professional astronomers have gazed at the night sky,

135
00:10:43,960 --> 00:10:45,560
hoping to find evidence of life.

136
00:10:46,440 --> 00:10:50,380
In the late 1800s, it appeared that one man had actually found it.

137
00:10:50,381 --> 00:10:54,420
Born into one of New England's wealthiest families,

138
00:10:54,421 --> 00:10:58,000
Percival Lowell was a Harvard educated mathematician

139
00:10:58,001 --> 00:11:01,440
turned US diplomat with a passion for astronomy

140
00:11:01,441 --> 00:11:05,260
and Mars in particular. In 1894,

141
00:11:05,261 --> 00:11:09,040
he used some of his personal fortune to build the Lowell Observatory

142
00:11:09,041 --> 00:11:14,220
in Flagstaff, Arizona and equipped it with the most advanced telescope of his day.

143
00:11:14,221 --> 00:11:18,000
He dedicated the facility to the study of Mars.

144
00:11:18,001 --> 00:11:27,300
Lowell published his findings in several books, and for the first time, the search for extraterrestrial life had become a widespread public sensation.

145
00:11:27,940 --> 00:11:30,140
But ultimately, it was all an illusion.

146
00:11:30,760 --> 00:11:36,020
Lowell made maps of Mars, which were, in many respects, good maps.

147
00:11:36,760 --> 00:11:45,420
But he was one of those observers who tend to see lines connecting the features of Mars, and Lowell saw lots and lots of these.

148
00:11:45,421 --> 00:11:49,860
and he went on to speculate that these were canals.

149
00:11:50,440 --> 00:11:55,640
His exploration of Mars suggested to him that there was a civilization there,

150
00:11:55,820 --> 00:11:58,700
that it was teetering on the brink of destruction,

151
00:11:59,320 --> 00:12:01,760
that Mars was a planet that was drying up,

152
00:12:01,761 --> 00:12:05,940
and that the people who lived there, or the entities that lived there,

153
00:12:06,320 --> 00:12:11,720
had created canals that would bring water from the poles to the various parts of the planet.

154
00:12:11,721 --> 00:12:18,800
It was never so much science as it was a scientifically based flight of imagination.

155
00:12:19,740 --> 00:12:22,800
Still, Lowell touched a nerve with the public,

156
00:12:23,520 --> 00:12:26,120
who were growing more and more fascinated with Martians.

157
00:12:26,920 --> 00:12:32,240
And on Halloween Eve 1938, that fascination led to sheer panic.

158
00:12:33,740 --> 00:12:37,960
Orson Welles' radio dramatization of novelist H.G. Welles' War of the Worlds

159
00:12:37,961 --> 00:12:44,920
convinced thousands of Americans that invaders from Mars were attacking Grover's Mill, New Jersey.

160
00:12:45,480 --> 00:12:50,940
I'm extremely surprised to learn that a story which has become familiar to children

161
00:12:50,941 --> 00:13:00,560
through the medium of comic strips and many succeeding novels and adventure stories

162
00:13:00,561 --> 00:13:05,760
should have had such an immediate and profound effect upon radio listeners.

163
00:13:05,761 --> 00:13:10,700
Following the War of the Worlds broadcasts and through the post-World War II years,

164
00:13:11,780 --> 00:13:16,100
Americans were reporting so many UFOs that in 1947,

165
00:13:16,920 --> 00:13:21,720
the Air Force initiated Project Blue Book to investigate and document the sightings.

166
00:13:22,260 --> 00:13:24,700
In the immediate post-World War II period,

167
00:13:25,380 --> 00:13:29,160
adults and children alike were in possession of these airplane cards

168
00:13:29,161 --> 00:13:31,500
which had silhouettes and they would look up in the sky

169
00:13:31,501 --> 00:13:34,580
and spot whether an airplane was friend or foe.

170
00:13:34,581 --> 00:13:44,400
And I suspect that had something to do with the UFO craze, because once people start looking up, expecting to see flying objects, and then if they see a light or

171
00:13:44,401 --> 00:13:49,280
something that they can't identify, it becomes an unidentified moving something.

172
00:13:50,240 --> 00:13:55,700
Walter Cronkite examined the UFO phenomenon in a 1966 primetime documentary.

173
00:13:55,701 --> 00:14:01,800
At the beginning of recorded time, men have been seeing unexplainable things in the sky.

174
00:14:02,340 --> 00:14:04,600
And there's no reason to doubt they saw something.

175
00:14:05,140 --> 00:14:08,020
The question is, was what they saw really there?

176
00:14:08,540 --> 00:14:10,540
And what was it they really saw?

177
00:14:11,480 --> 00:14:14,460
Cronkite and other reporters interviewed eyewitnesses,

178
00:14:14,540 --> 00:14:18,140
and even those claiming to have been on board flying saucers.

179
00:14:18,800 --> 00:14:23,740
And they had a compartment in the wall which both cleans and deodorizes their clothes

180
00:14:23,741 --> 00:14:25,780
by some light process,

181
00:14:26,100 --> 00:14:29,360
which required no water, soap or detergents

182
00:14:29,361 --> 00:14:30,380
or washing machines.

183
00:14:31,780 --> 00:14:33,960
Also interviewed was a young astronomer

184
00:14:33,961 --> 00:14:35,300
from Cornell University,

185
00:14:35,740 --> 00:14:37,580
who would go on to be a seminal figure

186
00:14:37,581 --> 00:14:40,160
in the scientific search for extraterrestrial life.

187
00:14:41,940 --> 00:14:44,820
But Carl Sagan felt the UFO phenomenon

188
00:14:44,821 --> 00:14:47,340
was rooted more in religion than science.

189
00:14:48,160 --> 00:14:51,320
It's a way of having beings that come from the sky

190
00:14:51,321 --> 00:14:52,960
that are worried about us, that are powerful.

191
00:14:53,740 --> 00:14:59,220
that are going to step in and prevent us from destroying ourselves, as we know it very well might,

192
00:14:59,940 --> 00:15:02,940
and yet have it in the cloak of science.

193
00:15:04,220 --> 00:15:08,800
Amidst charges by UFO witnesses of a government cover-up and conspiracy,

194
00:15:09,740 --> 00:15:13,360
the Air Force terminated Project Blue Book in December of 1969.

195
00:15:14,420 --> 00:15:18,960
It documented a total of 12,618 sightings.

196
00:15:19,780 --> 00:15:22,340
701 remained unidentified.

197
00:15:22,341 --> 00:15:26,540
When it comes to extraterrestrial life, I really don't believe that there's a cover-up here.

198
00:15:26,900 --> 00:15:29,460
To begin with, I'm not quite sure why the government would want to keep that secret.

199
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:32,720
But, hey, even if you want to believe that they would keep it secret,

200
00:15:33,040 --> 00:15:34,780
do you think that every government wants to keep it secret?

201
00:15:34,880 --> 00:15:36,800
Are the Bulgarians keeping it secret, right?

202
00:15:36,840 --> 00:15:39,160
Are the Czechs keeping it secret? Are the British keeping it secret?

203
00:15:40,520 --> 00:15:44,960
Or do the aliens only land in America, so only one government has to be worried about this?

204
00:15:46,100 --> 00:15:49,260
In 1960, during the height of the UFO frenzy,

205
00:15:49,261 --> 00:15:52,000
and when the space program was still in its infancy,

206
00:15:52,340 --> 00:15:57,900
A radio astronomer named Frank Drake gave the world its first legitimate scientific method

207
00:15:57,901 --> 00:16:00,660
of searching for extraterrestrial intelligence.

208
00:16:02,220 --> 00:16:06,600
Radio astronomy emerged as an important science in the late 1930s

209
00:16:06,601 --> 00:16:12,880
with the discovery that celestial objects like galaxies, their stars, and even planets

210
00:16:12,881 --> 00:16:18,140
emit naturally occurring radio waves that can be detected on Earth by radio telescopes.

211
00:16:18,141 --> 00:16:28,840
Always built in remote areas to minimize interference, a radio telescope's large dish collects the waves and bounces them up to a reflector.

212
00:16:29,800 --> 00:16:35,680
The reflector focuses the waves, then feeds them to a receiver located at the bottom of the dish.

213
00:16:36,840 --> 00:16:40,540
The receiver amplifies, then transmits the signal to computers.

214
00:16:40,541 --> 00:16:44,340
scientists can then analyze the radio waves

215
00:16:44,341 --> 00:16:47,540
and create visual representations or maps

216
00:16:47,541 --> 00:16:49,600
of the object from which they originated

217
00:16:49,601 --> 00:16:55,940
on earth man-made radio waves generated by radio and television broadcasts

218
00:16:55,941 --> 00:16:58,800
have been traveling through the universe for about a hundred years

219
00:16:58,801 --> 00:17:01,360
and could conceivably be detected

220
00:17:01,361 --> 00:17:03,820
by extraterrestrial civilizations

221
00:17:03,821 --> 00:17:06,780
that also have some form of radio technology

222
00:17:06,781 --> 00:17:11,140
and conversely, we could detect their transmissions.

223
00:17:11,880 --> 00:17:15,460
I think it's likely that all civilizations pass through an era

224
00:17:15,461 --> 00:17:17,680
as we are now passing through

225
00:17:17,681 --> 00:17:21,580
in which we have very high-power television broadcasts

226
00:17:21,581 --> 00:17:23,440
which can be detected by other civilizations.

227
00:17:23,880 --> 00:17:25,000
There will be such a period.

228
00:17:25,500 --> 00:17:27,900
Other civilizations will have their I love Lucy

229
00:17:27,901 --> 00:17:31,120
and we can capture it and it doesn't take much to do it.

230
00:17:32,020 --> 00:17:35,400
In 1960, Frank Drake was a radio astronomer

231
00:17:35,401 --> 00:17:38,600
at the newly built National Radio Astronomy Observatory

232
00:17:38,601 --> 00:17:41,040
in rural Greenbank, West Virginia.

233
00:17:42,120 --> 00:17:45,640
That year, Drake began aiming the observatory's radio telescope

234
00:17:45,641 --> 00:17:47,400
toward select stars

235
00:17:47,401 --> 00:17:51,600
in hopes of detecting radio waves that had an artificial signature,

236
00:17:52,520 --> 00:17:55,380
the broadcasts of an extraterrestrial civilization.

237
00:17:56,400 --> 00:17:58,380
When we were observing, we didn't listen

238
00:17:58,381 --> 00:18:00,960
because the signal that comes out of a radio telescope

239
00:18:00,961 --> 00:18:03,880
is just static and noise, unless there's really a signal there.

240
00:18:03,881 --> 00:18:06,180
but we had a pen and ink recorder.

241
00:18:06,900 --> 00:18:08,620
Then you watched the squiggly line,

242
00:18:08,760 --> 00:18:10,140
and you hoped that at some point,

243
00:18:10,920 --> 00:18:14,340
the pen drawing the squiggly line would jump over to one side,

244
00:18:14,620 --> 00:18:15,800
which is what you would get

245
00:18:15,801 --> 00:18:18,480
if there was actually a signal being detected.

246
00:18:19,900 --> 00:18:22,660
Drake named his experiment Project Ozma,

247
00:18:23,020 --> 00:18:24,880
after the fictional princess of Oz.

248
00:18:25,260 --> 00:18:27,540
The land of Oz was a land far away,

249
00:18:27,800 --> 00:18:29,200
very difficult to reach,

250
00:18:29,780 --> 00:18:32,560
and populated by strange and exotic beings.

251
00:18:32,561 --> 00:18:36,840
And so that's a good description of the place we're trying to find.

252
00:18:37,800 --> 00:18:41,080
While Osmond never detected an intelligent ET signal,

253
00:18:41,740 --> 00:18:45,140
it laid the foundation for what would become known as SETI,

254
00:18:45,720 --> 00:18:47,980
the Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence.

255
00:18:49,260 --> 00:18:53,060
SETI would come to be the acronym applied to all radio telescope searches

256
00:18:53,061 --> 00:18:55,580
for artificially generated radio waves.

257
00:18:56,860 --> 00:19:01,460
But in 1960, the public wasn't thinking about radio telescopes.

258
00:19:01,461 --> 00:19:07,120
If we wanted to meet E.T., we'd just blast off in a rocket and go see him.

259
00:19:08,380 --> 00:19:16,440
The world's first radio telescope was built by amateur astronomer Grote Reber in 1937 in his backyard in Wheaton, Illinois.

260
00:19:17,220 --> 00:19:20,240
He used it to make the first radio maps of the sky.

261
00:19:21,940 --> 00:19:24,420
E.T. Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

262
00:19:26,120 --> 00:19:29,480
We now return to E.T. Tech on Modern Marvels.

263
00:19:31,460 --> 00:19:36,700
The race to the moon highlighted America's space program in the 1960s.

264
00:19:37,220 --> 00:19:40,160
But even during the earliest days of the program,

265
00:19:40,900 --> 00:19:43,500
NASA was also pursuing planetary exploration

266
00:19:43,501 --> 00:19:47,040
and the search for life using unmanned probes.

267
00:19:47,720 --> 00:19:50,960
The first series of planetary probes, named Mariner,

268
00:19:51,600 --> 00:19:54,800
were destined for our closest neighbors, beginning with Venus.

269
00:19:55,220 --> 00:19:56,960
Venus is covered with clouds.

270
00:19:56,961 --> 00:20:01,960
So for many decades, observers could tell almost nothing about it.

271
00:20:01,961 --> 00:20:06,500
In that absence of evidence, a lot of speculation flooded in about how well,

272
00:20:06,501 --> 00:20:09,900
if it's cloudy, Venus must be like a rain forest,

273
00:20:09,901 --> 00:20:15,720
and under there would be these wonderful tropical forests and abundant life forms and so forth.

274
00:20:16,940 --> 00:20:20,960
Mariner 1 was launched on July 22, 1962.

275
00:20:20,961 --> 00:20:25,960
Its mission was to survey Venus' atmospheric and surface conditions.

276
00:20:26,960 --> 00:20:35,400
Five minutes after launch, the rocket carrying it into space went off course.

277
00:20:36,080 --> 00:20:39,640
So ground controllers blew it up, destroying Mariner 1 as well.

278
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:48,100
Mariner 2 was successfully launched five weeks later.

279
00:20:48,800 --> 00:20:55,920
After a four-month journey, it became the first spacecraft to ever encounter another planet when it began orbiting Venus.

280
00:20:56,960 --> 00:21:01,320
As it was not equipped with cameras, these images were compiled from a later probe.

281
00:21:02,200 --> 00:21:07,140
But Mariner 2's sensors indicated that Venus was an inhospitable planet for life.

282
00:21:07,940 --> 00:21:11,600
It has what scientists call a runaway greenhouse effect,

283
00:21:11,601 --> 00:21:18,140
with a thick carbon dioxide atmosphere and surface temperatures of 900 degrees Fahrenheit.

284
00:21:19,160 --> 00:21:23,220
The search for life then moved to our next closest neighbor, Mars.

285
00:21:23,221 --> 00:21:30,360
On July 15, 1965, Mariner 4 flew within 6,100 miles of Mars

286
00:21:30,361 --> 00:21:35,080
and began transmitting the first close-up pictures of the red planet back to Earth.

287
00:21:35,600 --> 00:21:37,360
What it showed us was surprising.

288
00:21:38,140 --> 00:21:41,140
It saw a Mars landscape that reminded us more of the moon.

289
00:21:41,880 --> 00:21:45,740
All of this astronomy, careful thinking, the best measurements we could make from Earth

290
00:21:45,741 --> 00:21:49,720
had shown us a Mars that was dynamic, exciting, possibly life-bearing.

291
00:21:49,721 --> 00:21:53,980
And then we saw the lunar face, not a place you'd expect to find life.

292
00:21:55,260 --> 00:21:56,720
But not everyone was disillusioned.

293
00:21:58,120 --> 00:22:02,100
Mariner 4 returned a total of 21 grainy black-and-white photos,

294
00:22:02,660 --> 00:22:05,160
covering less than 1% of the planet's surface.

295
00:22:06,940 --> 00:22:10,080
A more advanced probe was launched in 1971.

296
00:22:11,560 --> 00:22:14,560
Mariner 9 would photograph nearly the entire surface.

297
00:22:15,240 --> 00:22:18,940
But it arrived at Mars in the middle of a massive dust storm.

298
00:22:18,941 --> 00:22:22,660
We actually had to wait three months before we ever took a photograph of the surface.

299
00:22:23,180 --> 00:22:25,680
Mariner 9 happily waited as the dust storm subsided,

300
00:22:26,140 --> 00:22:30,600
and the first land it saw was the peak of a 90,000-foot volcano.

301
00:22:31,940 --> 00:22:35,140
The gigantic volcano named Olympus Mons,

302
00:22:35,620 --> 00:22:40,300
shown here as photographed by Mariner 9 and then by a more advanced probe years later,

303
00:22:40,860 --> 00:22:44,140
is the largest known volcanic edifice in our solar system.

304
00:22:45,040 --> 00:22:48,940
Having transmitted more than 7,000 photographs back to Earth,

305
00:22:49,800 --> 00:22:53,160
Mariner 9 provided a comprehensive view of the planet's surface

306
00:22:53,161 --> 00:22:56,660
using higher resolution photography than prior probes.

307
00:22:57,320 --> 00:23:02,780
In addition to volcanoes, Mariner 9 also revealed the Val Marinaris,

308
00:23:03,440 --> 00:23:08,900
a 3.5-mile deep canyon, long enough to stretch from New York to California,

309
00:23:09,520 --> 00:23:11,460
and numerous dry river channels.

310
00:23:12,440 --> 00:23:20,060
The combination of internal heat generated by volcanoes and flowing water would have provided ideal elements for past life.

311
00:23:21,140 --> 00:23:25,820
Now scientists had to figure out whether it was still present and how to find it,

312
00:23:26,340 --> 00:23:28,840
or look for evidence of its extinction.

313
00:23:29,800 --> 00:23:32,040
They would get that chance with Project Viking.

314
00:23:32,660 --> 00:23:37,360
Viking is the big enchilada when it comes to a Mars mission.

315
00:23:37,361 --> 00:23:42,360
This was a major effort. It included two landers and two orbiters.

316
00:23:42,361 --> 00:23:44,360
Multi-billion dollar program.

317
00:23:44,361 --> 00:23:51,360
The Viking landers were literal robotic laboratories designed to conduct numerous tests of the Martian surface.

318
00:23:51,361 --> 00:23:58,360
They could look for evidence of internal activity through seismographs, conduct five life-oriented experiments,

319
00:23:58,361 --> 00:24:04,360
looking for the chemistry of life and how life might metabolize in a Martian system, how it might produce chemicals.

320
00:24:04,361 --> 00:24:11,927
produce chemicals. Viking 1 touched down at its landing site on July 20th, 1976.

321
00:24:11,928 --> 00:24:13,440
Viking 2 arrived

322
00:24:13,441 --> 00:24:14,593
four months later.

323
00:24:14,594 --> 00:24:19,720
The immense thrill of actually landing two sophisticated laboratories on another

324
00:24:19,721 --> 00:24:23,571
planet soon gave way to a sobering reality.

325
00:24:23,572 --> 00:24:27,960
Both landers scooped up and analyzed Martian soil

326
00:24:27,961 --> 00:24:29,360
through a variety of tests.

327
00:24:30,260 --> 00:24:33,360
But the tested samples showed not even a trace of carbon,

328
00:24:33,980 --> 00:24:36,040
the dominant element in the chemistry of life.

329
00:24:37,680 --> 00:24:40,740
In fact, scientists concluded that the surface of Mars

330
00:24:40,741 --> 00:24:44,700
was self-sterilizing due to the intense levels of radiation

331
00:24:44,701 --> 00:24:48,080
it's exposed to from the sun and deep space.

332
00:24:49,140 --> 00:24:52,120
The Viking landers, which still remain on the planet,

333
00:24:52,820 --> 00:24:54,880
failed to accomplish one important goal

334
00:24:54,881 --> 00:24:56,420
that disturbed many scientists.

335
00:24:56,980 --> 00:25:00,940
We never actually touched and analyzed a rock.

336
00:25:01,640 --> 00:25:05,360
The little chips of things we thought were rocks in the soil we grabbed ended up being dirt clods.

337
00:25:05,860 --> 00:25:09,860
So we did not analyze with this incredible gear rocks.

338
00:25:10,220 --> 00:25:15,460
Well, as I tell kids when I get a chance, rocks are Mother Nature's artifacts.

339
00:25:15,461 --> 00:25:21,540
They tell us the story about how planets are made, environments, what environments the rocks are formed in.

340
00:25:21,900 --> 00:25:23,080
So we've got to get to the rocks.

341
00:25:23,081 --> 00:25:30,660
In August 1996, 20 years after Viking, it appeared that the rock had come to us.

342
00:25:31,780 --> 00:25:38,680
NASA officials announced that a Mars meteorite found in Antarctica contained possible evidence of ancient Martian life.

343
00:25:39,620 --> 00:25:48,960
It's believed the four-pound rock was formed about 4.5 billion years ago, when Mars was warmer and probably contained large bodies of water.

344
00:25:48,961 --> 00:26:02,961
Then, about 15 million years ago, a large asteroid struck Mars and jettisoned the rock into space where it remained, until it landed in Antarctica in about 11,000 B.C.

345
00:26:03,160 --> 00:26:09,540
A team of scientists doing analysis at a microscopic level, really an electron microscopic level,

346
00:26:10,040 --> 00:26:18,020
found what they think are the remnants of some sort of microbial bacteria that looks fossilized.

347
00:26:18,760 --> 00:26:24,940
The result of that was a furor that existed in the scientific community for some time,

348
00:26:25,020 --> 00:26:29,400
and a debate that still is taking place in the scientific community over whether or not they're right.

349
00:26:29,401 --> 00:26:35,980
The largest of the possible fossils are less than 1 100th of the diameter of a human hair.

350
00:26:36,720 --> 00:26:41,740
And their appearance is similar to microscopic fossils of the tiniest bacteria found on Earth.

351
00:26:43,620 --> 00:26:47,700
Plans for the first missions back to Mars since Viking had already been drafted

352
00:26:47,701 --> 00:26:52,580
and were in the testing phase when the discovery of the Mars rock was made public.

353
00:26:52,581 --> 00:26:56,340
Three, two, one, release the atom.

354
00:26:56,341 --> 00:27:02,020
But now those missions were energized by the renewed possibility of finding life.

355
00:27:02,920 --> 00:27:05,320
One of the most ambitious was Pathfinder.

356
00:27:06,220 --> 00:27:08,780
Like Viking, it would send a lander to Mars.

357
00:27:09,420 --> 00:27:15,260
Unlike Viking, which was stationary, Pathfinder would include a six-wheeled rover called Sojourner.

358
00:27:15,261 --> 00:27:18,080
So by being mobile, we can go and touch the rocks.

359
00:27:18,580 --> 00:27:20,960
We didn't get to them on Viking, even though they were all around us.

360
00:27:21,620 --> 00:27:25,400
And that is part of NASA's newly crystallized Mars strategy.

361
00:27:26,340 --> 00:27:35,340
Launched November 4, 1996, Pathfinder landed on Mars on July 4, 1997.

362
00:27:35,341 --> 00:27:45,340
The next day, to the thrill of its jet propulsion laboratory handlers, Sojourner rolled off the Pathfinder lander and began roaming the Martian surface.

363
00:27:45,341 --> 00:27:51,340
Like all the missions before it, Pathfinder turned up no signs of life.

364
00:27:51,341 --> 00:27:56,040
But its true accomplishment was to prove the viability of the technologies necessary

365
00:27:56,041 --> 00:28:00,060
for more advanced missions, like those of Spirit and Opportunity.

366
00:28:00,220 --> 00:28:05,820
We have ignition and liftoff of Atlantis and the Galileo spacecraft bound for Jupiter.

367
00:28:07,480 --> 00:28:12,500
While much of the search for extraterrestrial life in our solar system has focused on Mars,

368
00:28:13,360 --> 00:28:18,000
NASA has launched a small armada of probes to seek evidence of life elsewhere.

369
00:28:18,960 --> 00:28:21,800
One of the most significant was Galileo.

370
00:28:22,860 --> 00:28:26,520
Launched in 1989 from the payload bay of the space shuttle Atlantis,

371
00:28:26,820 --> 00:28:29,640
its mission was to study Jupiter and its moons.

372
00:28:31,280 --> 00:28:34,080
Run by a computer less powerful than a Palm pilot,

373
00:28:34,920 --> 00:28:39,460
Galileo experienced a number of technical difficulties on its six-year journey to Jupiter.

374
00:28:40,500 --> 00:28:45,300
But when it began orbiting the gaseous planet 318 times the size of Earth,

375
00:28:45,500 --> 00:28:47,700
it sent back brilliant images.

376
00:28:48,000 --> 00:28:55,000
More significant is what it found when it orbited one of Jupiter's moons, Europa.

377
00:28:55,001 --> 00:29:01,000
It provided photographic evidence that salty oceans exist beneath the moon's icy surface.

378
00:29:01,001 --> 00:29:07,000
Scientists now believe life may very well be discovered there, based on where we found it on Earth.

379
00:29:07,001 --> 00:29:13,000
For instance, Lake Vostok is a sub-glacial lake in Antarctica.

380
00:29:13,001 --> 00:29:18,000
This lake is under 3.7 kilometers of ice.

381
00:29:18,001 --> 00:29:25,000
It's been isolated from the outside world for maybe 30 million years.

382
00:29:25,001 --> 00:29:31,000
And from samples of ice, we find microbes that are from the lake.

383
00:29:31,001 --> 00:29:36,000
So this isolated environment has life in it.

384
00:29:36,001 --> 00:29:42,000
Based on Galileo's findings, plans are already underway to develop GIMO,

385
00:29:42,001 --> 00:29:48,740
or the Jupiter Icy Moon Orbiter, to gain a deeper understanding of Europa's subsurface ocean.

386
00:29:50,140 --> 00:29:54,980
Galileo also raised hopes that life might exist on another of Jupiter's moons, Io.

387
00:29:55,780 --> 00:30:02,060
Rather than harboring a salty ocean, Galileo showed Io as a place of huge erupting volcanoes,

388
00:30:03,020 --> 00:30:05,080
another environment where life could exist.

389
00:30:05,940 --> 00:30:10,440
And it's the very possibility of life that led to Galileo's destruction.

390
00:30:10,441 --> 00:30:18,440
In September 2003, Galileo was transmitting the last of some 14,000 images back to Earth.

391
00:30:19,060 --> 00:30:22,280
It had outlived its original mission specs by six years.

392
00:30:23,080 --> 00:30:28,320
But it was also running out of the propellant it used to keep its antenna pointed toward Earth,

393
00:30:28,800 --> 00:30:30,920
so that engineers could control its course.

394
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:39,000
Once that control was lost, NASA feared Galileo could find itself on a collision course with Europa,

395
00:30:39,001 --> 00:30:42,960
and that it might still be carrying microorganisms from Earth.

396
00:30:43,400 --> 00:30:47,700
If you look at the ethical reasons for keeping microbes off of other planets,

397
00:30:48,280 --> 00:30:52,140
you essentially don't want to despoil other bodies that may have their own life

398
00:30:52,141 --> 00:30:55,100
and have Earth life come into conjunction with that other life.

399
00:30:55,980 --> 00:30:59,400
It's very important that if we go out to learn about a place

400
00:30:59,401 --> 00:31:03,300
that we not find the contamination that we brought ourselves.

401
00:31:03,940 --> 00:31:07,100
So controllers transmitted instructions to Galileo

402
00:31:07,101 --> 00:31:15,080
to change course back toward Jupiter and to then take a 108,000-mile-per-hour suicide plunge

403
00:31:15,081 --> 00:31:17,440
into the planet's dense atmosphere.

404
00:31:18,380 --> 00:31:22,820
It would be crushed and vaporized by heat and friction before reaching the surface.

405
00:31:25,520 --> 00:31:30,300
While NASA probes searched for signs of simple life forms within our solar system,

406
00:31:30,940 --> 00:31:37,200
In 1992, the agency initiated a plan to search for intelligent life beyond it.

407
00:31:38,860 --> 00:31:43,120
Olympus Mons, the huge Martian volcano, is three times the height of Mount Everest.

408
00:31:43,740 --> 00:31:46,140
Its base covers an area the size of Arizona.

409
00:31:47,520 --> 00:31:49,940
E.T. Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

410
00:31:52,320 --> 00:31:55,800
We now return to E.T. Tech on Modern Marvels.

411
00:32:00,300 --> 00:32:08,180
Nearly all NASA's probes have searched planets in our solar system for signs of life most

412
00:32:08,181 --> 00:32:13,060
likely to be microbial in form.

413
00:32:13,061 --> 00:32:18,980
But four probes launched in the 1970s, all carrying messages for extraterrestrials, continue

414
00:32:18,981 --> 00:32:22,540
making their way into deep space.

415
00:32:22,541 --> 00:32:29,260
Pioneer 10 and Pioneer 11 surveyed several planets, then were sent beyond our solar system,

416
00:32:29,261 --> 00:32:32,500
conceivably they could someday encounter an intelligent life form.

417
00:32:33,820 --> 00:32:38,780
With that in mind, NASA attached a gold-plated plaque to each probe,

418
00:32:39,360 --> 00:32:41,420
a greeting card for extraterrestrials.

419
00:32:41,900 --> 00:32:46,040
It was designed by Carl Sagan, his wife Linda, and Frank Drake.

420
00:32:46,520 --> 00:32:50,060
We decided we would draw a map showing the location of the Earth

421
00:32:50,061 --> 00:32:54,120
with respect to some identifiable objects, pulsars in fact,

422
00:32:54,860 --> 00:32:58,100
and give sketches of human beings.

423
00:32:59,260 --> 00:33:05,000
Pioneer 10 sent its final radio signal back to Earth on January 23, 2003.

424
00:33:05,900 --> 00:33:12,120
It was 7.6 billion miles away and continuing its journey out of our solar system.

425
00:33:12,640 --> 00:33:19,420
Two additional probes that would also leave our solar system, Voyager 1 and 2, were launched in 1977.

426
00:33:20,740 --> 00:33:27,060
This time, NASA created and attached gold-plated records full of images and sounds from Earth.

427
00:33:27,061 --> 00:33:31,060
Hello from the children of planet Earth.

428
00:33:31,061 --> 00:33:34,060
We wanted to show the good side of us.

429
00:33:34,061 --> 00:33:37,060
So there's no war picture, there's no nuclear bomb picture,

430
00:33:37,061 --> 00:33:40,060
there's no dentist drilling picture.

431
00:33:40,061 --> 00:33:46,060
So in a way the record doesn't give quite a balanced picture of our civilization.

432
00:33:46,061 --> 00:33:50,060
NASA's probes may have been the luminaries in the search for extraterrestrial life

433
00:33:50,061 --> 00:33:53,060
during the second half of the 20th century.

434
00:33:53,061 --> 00:33:58,340
But SETI, or Radio Telescope Searches for Extraterrestrial Intelligence,

435
00:33:58,341 --> 00:34:04,460
had been steadily expanding ever since Frank Drake started Project OSMA in 1960.

436
00:34:04,461 --> 00:34:07,780
Our spacecraft that go to the planet look for primitive life.

437
00:34:08,720 --> 00:34:11,560
With the radio search, we're looking for advanced life,

438
00:34:11,740 --> 00:34:13,560
and it's surely not in our solar system.

439
00:34:14,260 --> 00:34:15,960
But it could be in our galaxy.

440
00:34:17,060 --> 00:34:19,160
Shortly after initiating Project OSMA,

441
00:34:19,161 --> 00:34:23,360
Frank Drake devised a formula that scientists still use

442
00:34:23,361 --> 00:34:29,040
to calculate the potential number of detectable intelligent civilizations in our Milky Way galaxy.

443
00:34:29,940 --> 00:34:33,200
I sat down and wrote down all the things you need to know about

444
00:34:33,201 --> 00:34:37,300
to predict the number of detectable civilizations in the galaxy.

445
00:34:37,920 --> 00:34:39,360
I wrote them all down and I realized,

446
00:34:39,480 --> 00:34:42,680
well, if you multiply them all together, you get the number of detectable civilizations.

447
00:34:43,380 --> 00:34:45,360
The Drake equation is this.

448
00:34:46,320 --> 00:34:48,740
Take the number of stars in the Milky Way,

449
00:34:49,160 --> 00:34:51,880
times the fraction of stars with planetary systems,

450
00:34:52,760 --> 00:34:56,440
times the number of planets per solar system that are suitable for life,

451
00:34:57,380 --> 00:35:01,540
times the fraction of suitable planets on which life might actually appear,

452
00:35:02,520 --> 00:35:06,920
times the fraction of life-bearing planets on which intelligent life emerges,

453
00:35:08,140 --> 00:35:11,700
times the fraction of civilizations that develop a detectable technology,

454
00:35:12,580 --> 00:35:18,300
times the length of time such civilizations release detectable signals into space.

455
00:35:19,160 --> 00:35:22,060
There is no correct answer.

456
00:35:22,920 --> 00:35:26,340
The equation is devised for scientists to insert their own numbers

457
00:35:26,341 --> 00:35:28,180
and to draw their own conclusions.

458
00:35:28,980 --> 00:35:33,220
If you put in my numbers, they predict that about once per year in our galaxy

459
00:35:33,221 --> 00:35:35,460
a new technical civilization arises.

460
00:35:36,500 --> 00:35:39,020
And that may sound like an awful lot,

461
00:35:39,100 --> 00:35:44,540
but this is one out of 200 billion stars each year produces a new civilization.

462
00:35:44,541 --> 00:35:47,340
So it's not all that wild.

463
00:35:47,341 --> 00:35:49,403
At the same time, it's very exciting.

464
00:35:49,404 --> 00:35:55,480
It means there have been lots and lots of them produced, and literally billions in the history of the galaxy.

465
00:35:56,640 --> 00:36:06,100
Even as more conservative astronomers calculated the Drake equation, the belief increased that huge numbers of detectable civilizations exist in our galaxy.

466
00:36:07,960 --> 00:36:12,720
Several university and private observatories began conducting their own SETI projects.

467
00:36:12,721 --> 00:36:20,040
In 1984, the SETI Institute was formed to advance and coordinate SETI research.

468
00:36:20,780 --> 00:36:26,060
Then, eight years later, on the 500th anniversary of Columbus's arrival in the New World,

469
00:36:26,820 --> 00:36:31,600
NASA began funding its own SETI project, in cooperation with the SETI Institute.

470
00:36:32,760 --> 00:36:38,360
It utilized NASA's Deep Space Network telescopes that were usually used to track satellites

471
00:36:38,361 --> 00:36:42,740
and the enormous Arecibo radio telescope.

472
00:36:43,600 --> 00:36:45,840
Built in a jungle valley in Puerto Rico,

473
00:36:46,540 --> 00:36:50,140
Arecibo is the world's largest and most sensitive radio telescope.

474
00:36:51,040 --> 00:36:55,920
Its dish is 1,000 feet in diameter, and its reflector weighs 900 tons.

475
00:36:56,540 --> 00:36:59,920
We started down what we thought was going to be a 10-year-long path

476
00:36:59,921 --> 00:37:03,460
to survey 1,000 nearby stars and to survey the whole sky.

477
00:37:04,080 --> 00:37:11,840
The amendment I am offering today will eliminate funding for what I believe to be a foolish and wasteful NASA program.

478
00:37:11,841 --> 00:37:20,080
Less than one year later in 1993, Congress shocked the SETI community by terminating the program's $10 million per year average budget.

479
00:37:20,560 --> 00:37:22,840
Overnight, NASA was out of the SETI business.

480
00:37:23,180 --> 00:37:30,300
The problem that existed was members of Congress very much questioned what they referred to as the search for little green men.

481
00:37:30,301 --> 00:37:35,080
and specifically some members of Congress made this a crusade.

482
00:37:35,840 --> 00:37:41,820
Rather than accept defeat, the Institute began a fundraising campaign led by Barney Oliver.

483
00:37:42,660 --> 00:37:46,820
Oliver was an executive at Hewlett-Packard who held more than 60 patents

484
00:37:46,821 --> 00:37:52,380
and among other things is credited with the development of the handheld scientific calculator.

485
00:37:53,400 --> 00:37:58,460
While on leave of absence from the company, he headed NASA's short-lived SETI program.

486
00:37:58,461 --> 00:38:03,760
Barney just decided that this was something he was going to take on.

487
00:38:04,400 --> 00:38:08,140
And he took on a new career as a fundraiser.

488
00:38:09,100 --> 00:38:12,280
Oliver's fundraising effort resulted in Project Phoenix.

489
00:38:12,940 --> 00:38:16,360
Phoenix rising from the ashes of congressional termination.

490
00:38:17,360 --> 00:38:21,840
Today, Project Phoenix examines a thousand nearby sun-like stars,

491
00:38:22,520 --> 00:38:25,100
utilizing the Arecibo telescope, among others.

492
00:38:25,101 --> 00:38:27,780
several weeks a year

493
00:38:27,781 --> 00:38:30,660
city institute scientists visit arecibo

494
00:38:30,661 --> 00:38:34,040
and work in round the clock ships to conduct the search

495
00:38:34,041 --> 00:38:37,540
the popular image of what it's like to go to the telescope

496
00:38:37,541 --> 00:38:40,380
and listen for et is probably

497
00:38:40,381 --> 00:38:43,120
best illustrated by what many people saw in the movie contact

498
00:38:43,121 --> 00:38:46,700
where jodie foster sits on the hood of her car with a pair of earphones

499
00:38:46,701 --> 00:38:50,120
kind of dozes off for 30 seconds and then hears something that sounds like a

500
00:38:50,121 --> 00:38:53,340
major accident in the philadelphia symphony orchestra's

501
00:38:53,341 --> 00:38:56,583
timpani section or something like that. That's not the way it is.

502
00:38:56,584 --> 00:38:58,180
We don't know quite where on the

503
00:38:58,181 --> 00:39:02,281
radio dial the signal might be coming in, so we monitor tens of millions of channels.

504
00:39:02,282 --> 00:39:02,982
So you can't

505
00:39:02,983 --> 00:39:04,293
wear tens of millions of earphones.

506
00:39:04,294 --> 00:39:06,340
That's terribly uncomfortable, not very effective.

507
00:39:07,080 --> 00:39:12,520
We sit there in front of a bank of computer screens, and the computers are doing the listening.

508
00:39:13,480 --> 00:39:17,043
If the computers decide that this signal looks pretty good, they'll beep at us.

509
00:39:17,044 --> 00:39:17,744
They'll get our

510
00:39:17,745 --> 00:39:20,956
attention. We will get involved at some point in the loop.

511
00:39:20,957 --> 00:39:23,020
But mostly, it's the machinery that's

512
00:39:23,021 --> 00:39:26,020
listening for ET.

513
00:39:26,021 --> 00:39:29,520
Compared to Frank Drake's first SETI search in 1960,

514
00:39:29,521 --> 00:39:35,520
the power of today's searches have increased by a factor of a hundred trillion.

515
00:39:35,521 --> 00:39:40,020
But in a universe with billions of galaxies containing billions of stars,

516
00:39:40,021 --> 00:39:45,020
that's still not powerful enough to search for life everywhere astronomers would like.

517
00:39:45,021 --> 00:39:51,020
There is, however, another power boost looming on the horizon.

518
00:39:51,021 --> 00:39:55,780
Radio and TV signals, our beacons to ETs, travel at the speed of light.

519
00:39:56,260 --> 00:39:58,820
They reach Mars in as little as four and a half minutes,

520
00:39:59,140 --> 00:40:02,420
and the nearest star besides our sun, in four years.

521
00:40:03,080 --> 00:40:05,380
ET Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

522
00:40:07,120 --> 00:40:10,500
We now return to ET Tech on Modern Marvels.

523
00:40:13,480 --> 00:40:17,740
Virtually all SETI Institute searches for extraterrestrial intelligence

524
00:40:17,741 --> 00:40:22,540
Scientists have relied on the ability to rent time on existing radio telescopes that are

525
00:40:22,541 --> 00:40:27,360
primarily being used for other types of astronomical research.

526
00:40:27,361 --> 00:40:33,360
Over the next few years, the Institute will begin conducting searches on their own telescopes,

527
00:40:33,361 --> 00:40:36,840
24 hours a day, 7 days a week.

528
00:40:36,841 --> 00:40:40,840
The range of those searches will also increase dramatically.

529
00:40:40,841 --> 00:40:47,600
This is all the result of a $12.5 million grant made by Microsoft co-founder Paul Allen

530
00:40:47,601 --> 00:40:52,600
to construct the Allen Telescope Array in Northern California.

531
00:40:52,601 --> 00:40:58,460
The Allen Array will consist of 350 small inexpensive radio telescope dishes

532
00:40:58,461 --> 00:41:02,920
linked together to form a sort of super radio telescope.

533
00:41:02,921 --> 00:41:07,160
The array won't offer the sensitivity of the giant Arecibo telescope

534
00:41:07,161 --> 00:41:09,820
but it has other advantages.

535
00:41:09,821 --> 00:41:12,760
Arecibo has the biggest collecting area.

536
00:41:12,761 --> 00:41:17,080
It can find a particular signal at a greater distance.

537
00:41:17,820 --> 00:41:21,060
But it can only look at one direction at a time.

538
00:41:21,640 --> 00:41:27,720
And so in the beam that Arecibo makes on the sky with its large single dish,

539
00:41:28,040 --> 00:41:29,800
I'm only going to have one star.

540
00:41:29,801 --> 00:41:34,100
But with the Allen Telescope Array, now I've sacrificed collecting area.

541
00:41:34,620 --> 00:41:40,540
And what I've gained is this ability to look at multiple directions at the same time

542
00:41:40,541 --> 00:41:43,500
and look at multiple stars and speed up my search.

543
00:41:44,360 --> 00:41:48,300
So that means now that we're putting an outboard motor on the Nina Pinta in Santa Maria.

544
00:41:48,420 --> 00:41:50,140
The chances of discovery begin to go up.

545
00:41:50,780 --> 00:41:54,080
And then after that, you know, we keep putting on more motors

546
00:41:54,081 --> 00:41:57,680
because technology, mostly computer technology,

547
00:41:57,780 --> 00:42:04,020
allows this basic array of antennae to get faster and faster in their scrutiny of the heavens.

548
00:42:05,760 --> 00:42:09,060
While SETI has always been associated with radio telescopes,

549
00:42:09,700 --> 00:42:13,520
the SETI Institute has recently added conventional telescopes to the search

550
00:42:13,521 --> 00:42:16,420
in a project called Optical SETI.

551
00:42:17,500 --> 00:42:20,460
At the Lick Observatory near San Jose, California,

552
00:42:21,240 --> 00:42:23,340
SETI scientists look for light beacons,

553
00:42:23,880 --> 00:42:27,040
perhaps high-powered lasers being flashed toward Earth

554
00:42:27,041 --> 00:42:29,180
as a signal from another civilization.

555
00:42:30,540 --> 00:42:32,560
Can we say why they would want to do that?

556
00:42:32,620 --> 00:42:34,080
Well, of course, that's alien sociology.

557
00:42:34,081 --> 00:42:36,120
We don't know a whole lot about alien sociology.

558
00:42:36,121 --> 00:42:40,140
So it's a little hard to kind of second guess why they might do that.

559
00:42:40,380 --> 00:42:45,580
But it would be very easy, perhaps easy to the point of being a high school science fair project for them,

560
00:42:45,800 --> 00:42:50,300
to mount a campaign of pinging the nearest 10,000 or 100,000 stars with lasers,

561
00:42:50,540 --> 00:42:52,200
hoping that somebody will see their signal.

562
00:42:53,300 --> 00:42:59,040
To detect such signals, a specially designed light pulse detector box is attached to the back of a telescope.

563
00:43:00,240 --> 00:43:03,240
And what happens is the telescope is just aimed at nearby stars,

564
00:43:03,241 --> 00:43:09,480
And that box is looking for quick flashes of light, not the steady star light that any telescope can see,

565
00:43:09,720 --> 00:43:14,820
but very short pulses of light, a billionth of a second or less, really quick flashes,

566
00:43:15,060 --> 00:43:17,220
because those are things that stars don't make.

567
00:43:18,280 --> 00:43:23,980
If the computers associated with either an optical SETI or a radio SETI search detect a signal,

568
00:43:24,900 --> 00:43:31,000
the scientists monitoring them would immediately conduct a series of tests to make sure the computers were correct.

569
00:43:31,820 --> 00:43:37,820
They would then ask observatories around the world to examine the signal themselves to confirm the finding.

570
00:43:38,660 --> 00:43:43,800
The first thing that we're going to do, if and when we get the confirmation that we found the signal,

571
00:43:44,360 --> 00:43:51,620
is we're going to open the bottle of champagne that's sitting in the refrigerator at Arecibo and do a little bit of celebrating.

572
00:43:53,400 --> 00:43:58,920
Whether newly discovered cosmic relatives will celebrate with us is still a great unknown.

573
00:43:58,921 --> 00:44:07,720
But the scientific search for extraterrestrial life will continue for as long as human beings ponder whether we're alone in the universe.

574
00:44:08,420 --> 00:44:14,760
The search may even outlive humanity itself in the form of the gold records on the Voyager probes,

575
00:44:14,920 --> 00:44:18,340
which will travel through space for centuries to come.

576
00:44:18,620 --> 00:44:22,020
About 5 billion years from now, the sun will expand, swallow up the earth.

577
00:44:22,420 --> 00:44:25,540
Everything we know will be vaporized.

578
00:44:26,240 --> 00:44:28,780
But a record of our existence will exist.

579
00:44:28,920 --> 00:44:31,780
For anybody who wants to learn about us,

580
00:44:31,900 --> 00:44:35,020
and that once upon a time, far away in the galaxy,

581
00:44:35,220 --> 00:44:36,540
there was a place called Earth,

582
00:44:36,820 --> 00:44:40,600
and a civilization run by human beings.

583
00:44:41,900 --> 00:44:45,980
And perhaps one other thing will be remembered about those human beings.

584
00:44:46,680 --> 00:44:50,880
That one of their greatest desires was to peer beyond the stars

585
00:44:50,881 --> 00:44:52,960
and contact a friend.

